  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Federal contracting in New Jersey has moved to $7B in obligations for FY2026 to date. The federal government recorded 350,592 awards this fiscal year to date, with an average award size of $20K. Obligations fell 24.8% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $4.5B. Lockheed Martin Corporation is the largest single vendor, receiving $1.9B in obligations. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences is the largest industry by obligations, at $1.4B. Recent fiscal years show higher totals, including $9.8B in FY24 and $9.3B in FY25, which provides context for the current-year decrease. These figures reflect contract obligations and the current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has federal contracting in New Jersey changed this fiscal year?",
        "body": "Total federal contract obligations for New Jersey are $7B for FY2026 to date. Obligations fell 24.8% year over year. Award activity includes 350,592 awards with an average award size of $20K. Recent year comparisons include FY25 at $9.3B and FY24 at $9.8B, which shows higher prior-year totals."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and industries dominate New Jersey federal contracting?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buyer with $4.5B in obligations. The Department of Veterans Affairs and the Department of Homeland Security follow with $846M and $596M respectively. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences leads industries with $1.4B, followed by Search, Detection, Navigation, Guidance, Aeronautical, and Nautical System and Instrument Manufacturing at $887M. Lockheed Martin Corporation is the top vendor at $1.9B."
      }
    ]
